Numbers and text without the C library: integers parsed from text (below), and floating point
to text: the shortest digits by Ryu, all others exact
(every decimal digit of the double, rounded to nearest, ties to even), digit for digit what
glibc's printf prints. Built on `1-primitives/floats/impl/` (shortest_decimal, exact_decimal).

- `sys::format_float(buffer, value, conversion, precision, alternate)` writes value as printf's
  `%f`, `%e`, `%g` or `%a` (and their capitals) would, with a precision when it is not negative
  and the `#` flag when alternate is set. Width and sign flags are the formatter's job.
- `sys::format_float_shortest(buffer, value)` writes the fewest `%g` digits that read back as the
  same value: `0.1` gives "0.1", not "0.10000000000000001". The `f32` overload round-trips as an
  `f32`, so `0.1F` also gives "0.1". This is what `{}` prints for a float.

Both write into a char span, NUL-terminate it (it must not be empty) and return the length,
at most `buffer.size() - 1`.

```c++
#include "ca/floats.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"
#include "ca/text.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	char buffer[32];
	ca::usz n = ca::sys::format_float_shortest(buffer, 0.1);
	if (ca::sv::str{buffer, n} != ca::sv::str{"0.1"})
		return 1;
	n = ca::sys::format_float_shortest(buffer, ca::f32{0.1F});
	if (ca::sv::str{buffer, n} != ca::sv::str{"0.1"})
		return 2;
	n = ca::sys::format_float(buffer, 3.14159, 'f', 2, false);
	if (ca::sv::str{buffer, n} != ca::sv::str{"3.14"})
		return 3;
	n = ca::sys::format_float(buffer, 1500.0, 'e', -1, false);
	return ca::sv::str{buffer, n} == ca::sv::str{"1.500000e+03"} ? 0 : 4;
}
```

The decimal point is always '.', whatever setlocale says. Two outputs differ from glibc on
purpose: `%#g` when rounding carries into a new exponent keeps all its digits, as C specifies
(glibc prints `1.e+02` for 99.5 at `%#.2g`, ca `1.0e+02`), and the shortest form of a
subnormal is truly the shortest (`5e-324`, where `%.15g` gave 15 digits).

### Integers from text

`sys::parse_integer<T>(text, base)` reads the whole of text as an integer of any integer type
but bool and the character types, in base 10 or any base 2 to 36: an optional `+` or `-` (`-`
for a signed T only) then digits, as `std::from_chars` takes them but with a `+` allowed and no
text left over. It fails with `errc::invalid_argument` for anything else (empty text, spaces,
an `0x` prefix, a stray character) and `errc::out_of_range` when the value does not fit in T,
the minimum of a signed T included. constexpr, header only.

```c++
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/text.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	auto status = ca::sys::parse_integer<int>("-42");
	if (!status || *status != -42)
		return 1;
	auto mask = ca::sys::parse_integer<ca::u32>("1ff", 16);
	if (!mask || *mask != 0x1FFU)
		return 2;
	auto big = ca::sys::parse_integer<ca::u8>("256");
	return !big && big.error() == ca::errc::out_of_range ? 0 : 3;
}
```
